e:["$","div",null,{"className":"hypothesis-explanation","children":"A capable agent may already work out the right approach from the sources. Preparing guidance and consulting it takes time and money. We need to find out whether these steps prevent enough mistakes to justify the effort."}]
f:["$","div",null,{"className":"hypothesis-foot","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"signal-dot"}],"The experiment is intended to answer this question. It has not answered it yet."]}]
10:["$","section",null,{"id":"example","className":"section-block","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"section-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"section-no","children":"01"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"GUIDANCE FOR A SPECIFIC TASK"}],["$","h2",null,{"children":"What would the layer tell an agent?"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"section-intro","children":"When an agent is given a task, it needs to understand how that task fits into the existing system. The layer should identify code the agent can reuse, explain which decisions constrain the change, and show where exceptions apply. The agent should be able to see the evidence behind each answer and whether it represents an approved decision, an observed pattern or a question that still needs review."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"section-intro","children":"The agent can consult the layer about a proposed approach: whether to extend an existing component, where new code belongs, or whether a change would cross a boundary the project has chosen to preserve. The answer should explain what is allowed and why, or identify the unresolved decision that needs an owner’s judgement. The agent uses those answers in its plan, which can then be checked against the relevant decisions before work proceeds."}],["$","$L16",null,{"children":["$","$L17",null,{"id":"guidance-examples","title":"See examples of the guidance an agent could receive","summary":"How this applies to change history, conflicting documentation and questions the code cannot answer.","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"NetBox is an open-source application used to document network infrastructure, including devices and their connections. It is one of the four projects we are examining in this study."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"In the first example, an agent is asked to write code that renames a device in NetBox. The layer would draw its attention to a related requirement: the change history must preserve the old name as well as the new one. It would also explain when NetBox already handles that requirement, so the agent can choose the right approach."}],["$","$L18",null,{}]]}]}]]}]
11:["$","section",null,{"className":"section-block","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"section-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"section-no","children":"02"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"THE IDEA BEHIND THE LAYER"}],["$","h2",null,{"children":"Turn what the project tells us into guidance for later work"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"section-intro section-intro-wide","children":"The playbook describes a process that goes from finding evidence to getting a decision approved and using it. The distinction matters: observing a pattern in code does not, by itself, make that pattern an approved rule."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mechanism","children":[["$","div","Find the evidence",{"className":"mechanism-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"step-top","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"M3 7V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2","key":"aa7l1z"}],["path",{"d":"M17 3h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v2","key":"4qcy5o"}],["path",{"d":"M21 17v2a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-2","key":"6vwrx8"}],["path",{"d":"M7 21H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-2","key":"ioqczr"}],["path",{"d":"M7 12h10","key":"b7w52i"}]],"className":"lucide-scan-line","size":22}],["$","span",null,{"children":["0",1]}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Find the evidence"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Read the relevant code, documentation and recorded decisions. Look for examples that follow the apparent rule and examples that do not."}]]}],["$","div","Explain and check the draft",{"className":"mechanism-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"step-top","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1:1:props:children:props:children:0:props:children:0:props:children:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-layers","size":22}],["$","span",null,{"children":["0",2]}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Explain and check the draft"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Write down the proposed rule, its evidence and exceptions. Automated checks verify the references and flag contradictions before a person reviews it."}]]}],["$","div","Ask an owner to review it",{"className":"mechanism-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"step-top","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"M3.85 8.62a4 4 0 0 1 4.78-4.77 4 4 0 0 1 6.74 0 4 4 0 0 1 4.78 4.78 4 4 0 0 1 0 6.74 4 4 0 0 1-4.77 4.78 4 4 0 0 1-6.75 0 4 4 0 0 1-4.78-4.77 4 4 0 0 1 0-6.76Z","key":"3c2336"}],["path",{"d":"m9 12 2 2 4-4","key":"dzmm74"}]],"className":"lucide-badge-check","size":22}],["$","span",null,{"children":["0",3]}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Ask an owner to review it"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"In the full playbook, someone responsible for the system decides whether the proposed guidance should be treated as an approved decision."}]]}],["$","div","Consult the layer and check the work",{"className":"mechanism-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"step-top","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["circle",{"cx":"6","cy":"19","r":"3","key":"1kj8tv"}],["path",{"d":"M9 19h8.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 0 0-7h-11a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 0-7H15","key":"1d8sl"}],["circle",{"cx":"18","cy":"5","r":"3","key":"gq8acd"}]],"className":"lucide-route","size":22}],["$","span",null,{"children":["0",4]}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Consult the layer and check the work"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The agent consults the layer before acting. Its plan names the relevant decisions, and review and automated checks assess whether the work follows them."}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"scope-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"What we can test here"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"We are using public projects and their published records, without asking their maintainers to take part. That lets us check whether guidance is supported by the evidence and helps with a change. It does not tell us whether an owner would approve a newly proposed rule, or what they intended but never recorded."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"working-loop","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"How we propose to check the agent in this POC"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Proposed pilot procedure — still to be built and tested."}]," In the interactive condition, a separate checking agent would assess the coding agent’s proposed approach and its resulting changes. It would use the same permitted evidence and the guidance prepared before the task, without access to the withheld answers."]}],"$L19","$L1a","$L1b","$L1c","$L1d"]}]]}]
12:["$","section",null,{"className":"section-block","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"section-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"section-no","children":"03"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"THE PROPOSED COMPARISON"}],["$","h2",null,{"children":"Separate the value of guidance from the value of checking its use"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"section-intro","children":"We should give agents the same change to make under three conditions. This would tell us whether preparing guidance helps by itself, and whether consulting the layer and checking the plan adds a further benefit. The pilot procedure above is our proposed starting point; the exact prompts, budgets and checks still need validation before the trial."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"comparison comparison-three","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"WITHOUT THE EXTRA LAYER"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"The agent works from the sources"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"It searches the code and available documentation, works out what matters for the task, and makes the change. This represents a capable agent doing the work directly."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"WITH PREPARED GUIDANCE"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"The agent receives the rules"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The layer has examined the same sources and written guidance before the task begins. The agent receives that guidance and works with it, without an interactive check by the layer."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"WITH GUIDANCE AND INTERACTION"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"The agent checks its proposed actions"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The agent receives the same guidance, consults the layer about its plan and responds to checks at agreed points. We record whether those interactions prevent mistakes or add unnecessary work."}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"caption","children":"All three conditions need the same permitted evidence and comparable resources, including opportunities for review. We will count the cost of preparing guidance and any additional checking. The two guidance conditions must use the same frozen rules, so a difference between them is not simply caused by one receiving better guidance."}]]}]
13:["$","section",null,{"className":"section-block","id":"method","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"section-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"section-no","children":"04"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"THE EXPERIMENT, STEP BY STEP"}],["$","h2",null,{"children":"How we intend to run the experiment"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"section-intro","children":"We will ask agents to make the same code change with different kinds of help. Then we will compare the work they produce and the effort it took. Here is the proposed sequence for one test."}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"method-steps","aria-label":"The four steps in one experiment","children":[["$","li",null,{"className":"method-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"method-step-number","aria-hidden":"true","children":"1"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Prepare a task we know how to assess"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-content","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"We choose a project rule with evidence we can inspect, then write a code-change task where that rule matters. Before an agent attempts it, we establish how to recognise a correct change, a real mistake and a valid exception. We keep this assessment outside the agent’s workspace."}],["$","$L16",null,{"children":["$","$L17",null,{"id":"choose-case","title":"How do we choose a task and establish the answer?","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"We look for a published requirement or decision, then check its scope against the implementation. If the guide and code disagree, the assessment must explain the disagreement. Copying the guide is not enough."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"We prepare a valid solution and a deliberately incorrect change, and check that our tests distinguish them. We also include a legitimate exception or alternative so the scoring does not demand one particular implementation. If the evidence cannot settle the answer, that part of the case remains unresolved."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The candidate projects are NetBox, Wagtail, Paperless-ngx and HTTPX. They offer different kinds of engineering decisions, but all four use Python and three use Django. They are not a representative sample of all software projects."}],["$","$L1e",null,{}]]}]}]]}]]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"method-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"method-step-number","aria-hidden":"true","children":"2"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Let the layer prepare its guidance"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-content","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The layer examines the allowed project material without seeing the change task. It drafts guidance, and a separate verifier checks whether the evidence supports it. We save the resulting guide before revealing the task, and keep it unchanged during the run."}],["$","$L16",null,{"children":[["$","$L17",null,{"id":"allowed-evidence","title":"Does the layer read the documentation, or work the rule out from code?","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"We test those two abilities separately."}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"plain-list","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Working it out from code."}]," We remove the documents and other statements that reveal the target rule. The layer has to use the remaining code evidence, or acknowledge that the rule cannot be established."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Using documentation."}]," We make the declared documents available. Here we assess whether the layer understands their scope, preserves exceptions and notices disagreements with the code."]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Each claim must say where it came from. A rule copied from a guide will not be reported as a rule inferred from code. The verifier uses only the material allowed in that test; it cannot consult our hidden assessment to repair the guide."}],"$L1f"]}],"$L20"]}]]}]]}],"$L21","$L22"]}],"$L23","$L24"]}]
14:["$","div",null,{"className":"route-footer","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"For the work completed so far and the decisions still ahead."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/interpretation-layer-poc/progress/","children":["Progress & findings ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"M5 12h14","key":"1ays0h"}],["path",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7","key":"xquz4c"}]],"className":"lucide-arrow-right","size":18}]]}]]}]
15:["$","footer",null,{"className":"site-footer","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"AI Engineering Playbook · Interpretation-layer validation"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Research in progress · Updated as the study develops"}]]}]
26:I["1c4abca9d553",[],"EvidenceDownloads",1]
27:I["1c4abca9d553",[],"EvidenceExplorer",1]
19:["$","ol",null,{"className":"numbered-detail","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Check the plan before the first edit."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The coding agent asks the layer what to reuse and which rules apply. It then submits the components it will change, the rules it will follow and any exception it intends to use. The checker assesses that reasoning, rather than merely looking for citations."}]]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Make objections lead to a recorded decision."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The checker returns “proceed”, “revise” or “unresolved”, with a reason. A request to revise goes back to the coding agent. The pilot proposal allows at most two correction rounds per checkpoint; an unresolved decision or an exhausted allowance stops the attempt and is recorded for later assessment."}]]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Check a changed approach before work continues."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"If the agent needs a new dependency, changes the component it plans to reuse, or relies on a new exception, it must submit an updated plan. The program running the experiment would also compare each submitted code change with the plan. A mismatch sends the attempt back for review."}]]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Judge the code independently at the end."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"All conditions run the same task checks. After the attempt ends, a separate evaluator assesses the final code against the reference answer. We count missed violations, unnecessary objections, unfinished attempts and checking effort. A “proceed” from the layer is not evidence that the change was correct."}]]}]]}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"children":"Agents working directly from the sources, and agents given prepared guidance, would also have their plans and code reviewed. Those reviews would happen at the same required points in the task. Each agent would have the same limit on how many times it could revise its work after an objection."}]
1b:["$","p",null,{"children":"For the agent working directly from the sources, the reviewer would use the allowed code and documentation. For the agent given guidance, the reviewer would also receive that guidance. Both reviewers could identify mistakes and request corrections. The interactive group would additionally have the layer ask targeted questions about how the proposed action follows a rule or qualifies for an exception."}]
1c:["$","p",null,{"children":"Giving every group a review makes the comparison more useful. If the interactive group does better, we can examine whether the layer’s specific questions helped, with ordinary review already available to the other groups. All reviewing and questioning would count towards the agreed resource budget."}]
1d:["$","p",null,{"className":"caption","children":"Owner approval of new decisions, renewal of guidance and checks across an organisation’s systems remain outside this pilot."}]
1f:["$","p",null,{"children":"Missing or incorrect guidance remains part of the result. We also assess unsupported claims and whether the layer recognises uncertainty. The website’s examples are illustrations, not the guidance we will give an experimental agent."}]
20:["$","$L17",null,{"id":"isolation","title":"How do we keep the withheld answers out of the run?","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Each attempt starts in a fresh container and fresh model conversations. Only declared files and network access are allowed. The reference answers and scoring stay outside. We record the actual model inputs, outputs and tool activity, and test that prohibited files and network destinations cannot be reached."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"We must also look for disclosures in comments, tests, examples and dependencies. Removing the obvious guide does not establish that its contents have been withheld."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Public code may already be familiar to a model from training. Some tests will therefore use unpublished changes that alter the correct rule. We check whether the answer follows the changed code. Harmless renaming should leave the interpretation unchanged. These controls can make the test inspectable; they cannot prove that the original project was absent from training data."}]]}]
21:["$","li",null,{"className":"method-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"method-step-number","aria-hidden":"true","children":"3"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Run the same task three ways"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-content","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Separate agent attempts receive the same task and the same allowed project sources. What changes is the help they receive:"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"method-groups","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Sources only."}]," The agent works out what matters from the code and available documentation."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Sources and guidance."}]," The agent also receives the guide prepared in step 2."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Sources, guidance and interaction."}]," The agent receives that same guide and consults the layer about its plan and actions."]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"children":"All three groups get ordinary review and opportunities to correct mistakes. The interactive group also receives targeted questions about how it applies the rules. The required review points and correction limits are the same across groups, and all reviewing counts towards the agreed budget."}],["$","$L16",null,{"children":["$","$L17",null,{"id":"during-work","title":"What does the checker do while the agent works?","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Before the first edit,"}]," the interactive agent consults the layer and submits a plan. It explains what it will reuse, what it will change and which rules or exceptions apply. A separate checker assesses whether those proposed actions follow the evidence."]}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"After a check,"}]," the agent receives “proceed”, “revise” or “unresolved”, with a reason. The proposed limit is two correction rounds at each required review point. An unresolved decision, or failure to satisfy the check within that allowance, stops the attempt. We retain unfinished work for assessment."]}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"If the approach changes,"}]," a new dependency, a different component to reuse or a new claimed exception requires an updated plan. The program controlling the run also compares submitted code with that plan. We must test that it catches undeclared changes of approach."]}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The checker can see the task, plan, changes and permitted evidence. It cannot see the hidden answers or the coding agent’s private reasoning. It cannot rewrite the frozen guidance. Questions needing an owner’s authority remain unresolved; we do not insert an unrecorded human answer during a run."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Reviewers in the other groups can still identify mistakes and request corrections. They use their group’s sources and, where supplied, guidance. The intended difference is the structured consultation and targeted questions, not the mere presence of a reviewer. Exact instructions and budgets still need to be specified and tested."}]]}]}]]}]]}]
22:["$","li",null,{"className":"method-step","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-heading","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"method-step-number","aria-hidden":"true","children":"4"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Judge the finished work and compare the cost"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"method-step-content","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"A separate judge assesses the final code against the assessment prepared in step 1. We compare correct changes, missed requirements, unnecessary objections and unfinished attempts. We also count the effort spent preparing guidance, reviewing work and answering questions."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The first comparison tells us whether prepared guidance helps. Comparing the two groups that receive guidance tells us whether interaction adds a further benefit."}],["$","$L16",null,{"children":[["$","$L17",null,{"id":"reliable-judge","title":"How do we know the judge is reliable?","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"We first develop its scoring instructions using examples whose answers have been established independently. Then we fix those instructions and test the judge on different decision families. Examples used to improve it cannot also serve as independent validation."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"It must catch convincing but incorrect work, accept valid exceptions and recognise when evidence is insufficient. We measure errors it accepts and correct solutions it rejects separately. We also test whether repeated grading or irrelevant changes in presentation alter its verdict. Group labels and model names are removed from final code scoring; interaction records are assessed separately."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Acceptable error rates and the amount of validation evidence must be fixed before validation results are seen. The four simple calibration records in the inventory are only starting material. We will audit scores during the pilot and revalidate any revised judge before rescoring all affected groups consistently."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The verifier, runtime checker and final judge each need to pass tests for their own role. Two AI models agreeing does not establish a reference answer. Without independent expertise to settle an ambiguous case, we must rely on reproducible behaviour or clear published evidence, or leave that judgement unresolved."}],["$","p",null,{"children":["The complete proposed qualification procedure is in section 9A of the ",["$","a",null,{"className":"text-link","href":"/interpretation-layer-poc/evidence/working-poc-plan.md","download":true,"children":"working plan"}],". It has not yet been carried out."]}]]}],["$","$L17",null,{"id":"interpret-results","title":"What would the comparison let us conclude?","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Useful guidance must be supported by evidence, improve later work and justify its cost. We will also assess the guidance itself, so a correct code change does not conceal unsupported rules. A separate small diagnostic supplies a known rule directly to check whether the task could benefit from correct guidance at all."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Several tasks may test the same underlying decision. We will account for that relationship rather than count every variation or repeated attempt as independent evidence. The final number of cases and minimum worthwhile improvement remain to be chosen before the main trial."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"A positive result would support the layer in the situations tested. It would not establish universal necessity, owner approval of new rules, adoption by a team or the value of keeping guidance current across an organisation. An inconclusive or negative result would also inform how we revise Chapter 4."}],"$L25"]}]]}]]}]]}]
23:["$","div",null,{"className":"method-preparation","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"We will test the procedure before using it to draw conclusions"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"We have written the component contracts and exercised one development case with scripted decisions and executable checks. Next we will implement isolated role execution, validate the components on separate material and run a small pilot. What we learn will help us fix the method and size of the main trial before it begins."}],["$","a",null,{"className":"text-link","href":"/interpretation-layer-poc/progress/","children":["See the current step and what remains ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$14:props:children:1:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-right","size":16}]]}]]}]
24:["$","$L16",null,{"children":["$","$L17",null,{"id":"method-records","title":"Read the full plan or inspect the source evidence","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The working plan records the proposed method, unresolved choices and changes to the design. The case library contains candidate material; it is not a finished benchmark."}],["$","$L26",null,{}],["$","$L27",null,{}]]}]}]
25:["$","a",null,{"className":"text-link","href":"https://yspbob.github.io/AI-Playbook/AI_Engineering_Playbook.html#4-the-interpretation-layer","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Read Chapter 4 in the public playbook ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$1:1:props:children:props:children:1:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":16}]]}]
